.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 125 PM PDT Thu Aug 27 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

Looking at satellite imagery, seeing more widespread mid-to-high
level cloud cover spreading across the Central Coast and southern
portion of the greater San Francisco Bay Area. The greatest
potential for high-based convection will be across the Central Coast
as the OAK Upper Air sounding reported MUCAPE (instability aloft)
slightly greater than 400 J/kg. This is also where the CAMs and
higher resolution deterministic models show the greatest MUCAPE
values today through early Friday morning. Thus, have added isolated
thunderstorms to the official forecast for the Central Coast. Further
north across the Bay Area, seeing less threat for thunderstorms, yet
remains a non-zero chance.

The Precipitable Water (PWAT) values increase to around or slightly
above 1.5" across the Central Coast by Friday morning with a greater
potential for wetting rains. However, rainfall amounts are projected
to largely be less than 0.10" throughout this event. Thus, we have
issued a Red Flag Warning for much of the Central Coast including
the Santa Cruz Mountains from 5 AM to 3 PM Friday. Fuels remain dry
from the recent warm and very dry conditions above the marine
influences, especially in areas above 1000 feet in elevation. This
is also where the CAMs have the greatest instability on Friday.
Given the Timber and Plaskett fires ongoing, any lightning strikes
and/or erratic/gusty outflows would make for difficult firefighting
efforts. Will closely monitor the latest forecast models and adjust
accordingly.

As the mid/upper level trough deepens off of the Pacific Northwest
coast Friday evening into Saturday morning, expecting this deeper
moisture plume to shift eastward resulting in PWAT values lowering
to near 0.50". Thus, ending the threat for high-based convection.
The mid/upper level low off of the Pacific Northwest will push
inland Friday night into Saturday, leading to a tightening pressure
gradient which will result in breezier winds Friday and into the
weekend. Thus, wind gusts are forecast to reach 25 to 35 mph along
the coast and through favored gaps, valleys, and passes. This
pattern will also usher in cooler temperatures. Zonal flow develops
over the weekend holding temperatures steadily below normal.

